    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "For their capstone project, Eva Orozco worked with a team of Stanford students through the class INTNLREL 192: Policy Practicum- Accountability in International Development to produce a research report for the Accountability Council (AC) in San Francisco on independent accountability mechanisms (IAMs) within multilateral development banks. Eva and their team were tasked with categorizing and analyzing information from 1,232 complaints raised to IAMs. The primary objective of this project was to identify areas for improvement within IAMs to enhance commitment implementation, address community needs, and maintain transparent and equitable accountability procedures for non-traditional complaint resolutions. The report enhanced Accountability Counsel’s goal of developing a deeper understanding of the most salient issues impacting complainants, along with creating a large language model with a large-scale, accurate training dataset. Ultimately, by providing specific and actionable data on the harms experienced by vulnerable communities, this report contributed to the research and activism needed to make meaningful impacts at both the community level by supporting client communities, and at the institutional level through policy advocacy.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Orozco, E. (2024). Coding Complaints to Independent Accountability Mechanisms to Understand Their Efficiency in Response Based on Region and Issue Type. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/vg708yw4665. https://doi.org/10.25740/vg708yw4665.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
